WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.026
Imagine a map where words occupy

00:00:03.046 --> 00:00:06.093
locations, and related words often sit

00:00:06.113 --> 00:00:07.175
near one another.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.245
Who drew the map?

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:11.390
Did someone label an axis meaning, another

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.147
axis emotion, and tell the computer where

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:15.616
every word belongs?

00:00:15.778 --> 00:00:16.788
Make a prediction.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.435
The chapter gives a more surprising route.

00:00:21.598 --> 00:00:24.090
Train a model to predict patterns in text.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.462
Adjust its parameters when predictions

00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:27.892
miss their targets.

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:30.457
Useful relationships can emerge in the

00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:33.124
resulting vectors without anyone drawing

00:00:33.144 --> 00:00:35.753
those relationships explicitly.

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:39.507
A representation is any form used to stand

00:00:39.527 --> 00:00:42.973
for an item. A vector is one form: an

00:00:42.993 --> 00:00:44.558
ordered list of numbers.

00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:47.071
Two numbers can specify a location on

00:00:47.091 --> 00:00:50.061
paper. Real language-model vectors often

00:00:50.081 --> 00:00:53.346
have hundreds or thousands of dimensions.

00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:56.236
An embedding is a mapping into a vector

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:59.265
space, usually learnt during training in

00:00:59.285 --> 00:01:00.836
modern language processing.

00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:02.989
We also call the output vector an

00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:05.270
embedding. A vector need not come from a

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.551
learnt mapping; these words do different

00:01:07.571 --> 00:01:08.452
jobs.

00:01:09.353 --> 00:01:12.600
BM twenty-five also calculates numbers.

00:01:12.762 --> 00:01:14.830
Its weights come from term counts,

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.536
document lengths and collection statistics

00:01:17.556 --> 00:01:19.314
through a specified formula.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.943
A neural encoder calculates an output from

00:01:21.963 --> 00:01:25.255
text, tokenisation and learnt parameters.

00:01:25.807 --> 00:01:27.698
The distinction is how the mapping was

00:01:27.718 --> 00:01:30.224
obtained. Training fits the encoder's

00:01:30.244 --> 00:01:32.685
parameters; inference uses the fitted

00:01:32.705 --> 00:01:34.656
mapping on new input.

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:36.616
A learnt calculation is still a

00:01:36.636 --> 00:01:39.257
calculation. Being able to reproduce its

00:01:39.277 --> 00:01:42.312
output does not make every dimension easy

00:01:42.332 --> 00:01:43.342
to interpret.

00:01:44.254 --> 00:01:45.480
Training needs a target.

00:01:45.642 --> 00:01:48.263
Where can one come from without a person

00:01:48.284 --> 00:01:50.970
labelling the meaning of every word?

00:01:51.158 --> 00:01:52.567
Hide part of a sentence.

00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:54.874
Ask the model to recover it.

00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:57.245
The original text supplies the answer.

00:01:57.806 --> 00:02:00.228
The distributional hypothesis suggests

00:02:00.248 --> 00:02:02.625
that words used in similar contexts tend

00:02:02.645 --> 00:02:04.519
to be used in similar ways.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:06.568
Predicting those contexts turns an

00:02:06.588 --> 00:02:09.236
observation about language into a training

00:02:09.256 --> 00:02:11.800
task. It does not supply a universal

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.076
definition of meaning.

00:02:13.994 --> 00:02:16.104
Word two vec has two familiar

00:02:16.124 --> 00:02:19.571
arrangements. Continuous bag of words, or

00:02:19.591 --> 00:02:22.690
C B O W, predicts a target word from its

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.112
neighbours. Skip-gram reverses that

00:02:25.132 --> 00:02:28.102
direction: use a target word to predict

00:02:28.122 --> 00:02:29.364
neighbouring words.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:32.183
Across many examples, predictions are

00:02:32.203 --> 00:02:34.580
compared with targets and parameters are

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.737
adjusted. After training, the word vectors

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.121
can be reused without running the original

00:02:40.141 --> 00:02:41.345
prediction task.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.935
Their dimensions are usually anonymous,

00:02:44.085 --> 00:02:46.088
and useful patterns are distributed across

00:02:46.108 --> 00:02:46.603
them.

00:02:47.524 --> 00:02:49.777
The famous demonstration takes king,

00:02:49.797 --> 00:02:52.522
subtracts man, and adds woman.

00:02:52.671 --> 00:02:54.777
In some trained spaces, queen is the

00:02:54.797 --> 00:02:56.942
nearest candidate after excluding the

00:02:56.962 --> 00:02:58.617
three input words.

00:02:58.779 --> 00:03:00.731
The step from man to woman resembles the

00:03:00.751 --> 00:03:02.586
step from king to queen.

00:03:03.155 --> 00:03:05.641
Our coordinates are deliberately invented

00:03:05.661 --> 00:03:08.669
to show that arithmetic exactly.

00:03:08.844 --> 00:03:10.473
They are not measurements from a trained

00:03:10.493 --> 00:03:13.050
model. Real analogies can fail.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:15.435
A successful example demonstrates a

00:03:15.455 --> 00:03:18.257
geometric regularity, not a universal

00:03:18.277 --> 00:03:19.919
equation about meaning.

00:03:20.832 --> 00:03:22.790
The chapter also shows selected

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.788
country-to-capital directions in a

00:03:24.808 --> 00:03:26.528
two-dimensional projection of

00:03:26.548 --> 00:03:28.279
thousand-dimensional vectors.

00:03:28.455 --> 00:03:30.587
That picture is a shadow of a larger

00:03:30.607 --> 00:03:33.667
space. Projection can discard differences

00:03:33.687 --> 00:03:34.607
that matter.

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:37.771
Our separate toy example makes the loss

00:03:37.791 --> 00:03:39.987
visible. Two three-dimensional points

00:03:40.007 --> 00:03:42.333
differ by ten units in their third

00:03:42.353 --> 00:03:45.116
coordinate. Drop that coordinate and both

00:03:45.136 --> 00:03:46.778
appear at the same location.

00:03:46.953 --> 00:03:49.458
This is a simple coordinate projection,

00:03:49.711 --> 00:03:52.500
not the chapter's P C A experiment.

00:03:53.411 --> 00:03:55.860
The geometry reflects patterns rewarded by

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.386
training. Predicting word contexts can

00:03:58.406 --> 00:04:01.105
produce useful relationships, but the task

00:04:01.125 --> 00:04:04.455
never directly asked which passage answers

00:04:04.475 --> 00:04:05.447
which question.

00:04:05.609 --> 00:04:07.819
Nearby does not automatically mean

00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:10.112
relevant to a particular search.

00:04:10.684 --> 00:04:13.329
Classic word vectors are a stepping stone.

00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:16.783
Modern encoders can read a whole sequence,

00:04:16.803 --> 00:04:19.334
produce context-dependent representations,

00:04:19.471 --> 00:04:21.731
and be trained for a retrieval task.

00:04:21.855 --> 00:04:24.374
To judge the resulting map, ask what it

00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:26.551
learnt to preserve, then test it on the

00:04:26.571 --> 00:04:28.523
queries and relevance judgments that

00:04:28.543 --> 00:04:29.218
matter.
